# x86 contract coverage — feature-by-feature audit

Logline: Map every top-level language feature (let, fn, match, if, while, for, struct, list, opt, int, float, string, fn call, return, rec fn, mut local) to the contract gate's coverage, the existence of an x86_emit_<feature>.oo module, and the empty-on-shape-mismatch policy, then rank the weakest.

Setup: x86_emit_contract.oo at ooda/oodac/x86_emit_contract.oo (204 lines) is the contract gate. The gate walks tokens for KW_FN, KW_REQUIRES, KW_ENSURES, KW_VERIFY, and IDENT "spec" only (lines 82, 89, 105, 118). No other language features are directly handled. The empty-on-shape-mismatch policy is the convention in the x86_emit_*.oo family: a matcher that does not see the exact shape it expects returns an empty list so the dispatch in x86_emit.oo can fall through to the next arm.

Beats:
  1. Build a 16-row coverage matrix of features × (contract, emit, empty).
  2. List features with 0/3 coverage (every cell N).
  3. List features with 1/3 coverage.
  4. Propose one contract-gate addition per uncovered feature.
  5. Rank the top 3 weakest features with a concrete action plan.

residual: Tier 5 #24 (typed-AST path) will subsume the token-walker chain. The contract gate's per-feature check list may collapse to a single uniform check after the typed-AST rollout.

File: openOODA/audit/x86-contract-coverage.oot
Status: open — Tier 6 #30 follow-up; contract gate is feature-thin.
Context: Read-only audit. No .oo files were modified.

---

## 1. Coverage matrix

The contract gate at ooda/oodac/x86_emit_contract.oo only processes KW_FN, KW_REQUIRES, KW_ENSURES, KW_VERIFY, and IDENT "spec". Every other language feature is not directly handled. The "emit" column asks whether a literal x86_emit_<feature>.oo file exists; the "empty-return" column asks whether the match function declares let empty and uses return empty on shape mismatch.

| #  | Feature    | Contract         | Emit file                                       | Empty | Evidence                                       |
|----|------------|------------------|-------------------------------------------------|-------|------------------------------------------------|
| 1  | let binding| N                | N (closest: x86_emit_typed_let.oo)              | N/A   | x86_emit_typed_let.oo:16-58                    |
| 2  | fn decl    | Y (KW_FN tracked)| Y (x86_emit_fn.oo)                              | Y     | x86_emit_fn.oo:20, 88, 143                     |
| 3  | match      | N                | Y (x86_emit_match.oo)                           | Y     | x86_emit_match.oo:40, 86                       |
| 4  | if         | N                | N (closest: x86_emit_ctl.oo)                    | N/A   | x86_emit_ctl.oo:19, 67, 174                    |
| 5  | while      | N                | N (closest: x86_emit_loop.oo)                   | N/A   | x86_emit_loop.oo:31                            |
| 6  | for        | N                | Y (x86_emit_for.oo)                             | Y     | x86_emit_for.oo:20                             |
| 7  | struct     | N                | Y (x86_emit_struct.oo)                          | Y     | x86_emit_struct.oo:55                          |
| 8  | list       | N                | Y (x86_emit_list.oo)                            | Y     | x86_emit_list.oo:29                            |
| 9  | opt        | N                | Y (x86_emit_opt.oo)                             | Y     | x86_emit_opt.oo:144                            |
| 10 | int        | N                | Y (x86_emit_int.oo)                             | Y     | x86_emit_int.oo:16                             |
| 11 | float      | N                | Y (x86_emit_float.oo)                           | Y     | x86_emit_float.oo:91 (uses List[Int])          |
| 12 | string     | N                | Y (x86_emit_str.oo)                             | Y     | x86_emit_str.oo:20, 82, 124                    |
| 13 | fn call    | N                | N (split: rsi/rdx/rcx/r89)                      | N/A   | x86_emit_rsi.oo:20, 104, 167                   |
| 14 | return     | N                | N (closest: x86_emit_ctl.oo:173 fn_const_ret)   | N/A   | x86_emit_ctl.oo:174                            |
| 15 | rec fn     | N                | Y (x86_emit_rec.oo)                             | Y     | x86_emit_rec.oo:18, 109                        |
| 16 | mut local  | N                | Y (x86_emit_mut.oo)                             | Y     | x86_emit_mut.oo:17                             |

Totals: 16 features surveyed. 1 with 3/3 (fn decl). 10 with 2/3. 0 with 1/3. 5 with 0/3.

Notes on the fn-decl "Y": the contract gate touches KW_FN at line 82 only to track fn_name so the next requires/ensures/spec/verify clause can be attributed. The fn body, parameters, and return type are not validated. Treat this as a context anchor, not a real coverage.

---

## 2. Features with 0/3 coverage (every cell N)

Five features have no contract coverage, no literal x86_emit_<feature>.oo file, and (by extension) no first-class empty-return policy:

1. **let binding** — closest file is x86_emit_typed_let.oo, but the matcher is scoped to `let ident: Int = V; println(ident)` only. Untyped let, multi-binding let, let with struct value, and let in a nested scope have no dedicated matcher and no contract check.
2. **if** — closest file is x86_emit_ctl.oo (control). It folds a const-equal `if` into one branch but has no general `if` matcher for runtime conditions. The contract gate does not detect KW_IF at all.
3. **while** — closest file is x86_emit_loop.oo. It matches `let mut i: Int = N; while i < M { ... i = i + 1; }` only. Other while shapes (no init, complex condition, break/continue) have no contract check and no generic matcher.
4. **fn call** — no single file. Arity is split across 1-arg (x86_emit_fn.oo:142), 2-arg (x86_emit_rsi.oo:166), 3-arg (x86_emit_rdx.oo), 4-arg (x86_emit_rcx.oo), 5+/6-arg (x86_emit_r89.oo). A 7+arg call, a call with a non-int/non-string arg, or a call with a struct arg has no matcher and no contract check.
5. **return** — closest file is x86_emit_ctl.oo:173 (fn_const_ret). The fn-body return shape in x86_emit_fn.oo:65 and x86_emit_rec.oo:41, 49 is shape-checked internally but not exposed as a generic return module, and the contract gate does not look at KW_RETURN.

---

## 3. Features with 1/3 coverage

None. Every feature that has a dedicated x86_emit_<feature>.oo file also has the empty-return policy. The contract gate never produces a 1/3 row in the current matrix; the minimum non-zero score is 2/3.

---

## 4. Recommended additions to the contract gate

One entry per §2 feature. Each addition names the token to detect inside x86_contract_walk_records (x86_emit_contract.oo:73) and the failure mode it would surface.

1. **let binding** — add a `KW_LET` arm after the `KW_FN` block at x86_emit_contract.oo:82. On detect, scan for the `;` and require the right-hand side to be a const-foldable literal INT, STRING, FLOAT, or a bare IDENT bound to a const above. Otherwise emit `ERR x86_emit contract not folded: let <name>`. Today a non-const let silently passes the gate and then trips one of the matcher arms downstream.
2. **if** — add a `KW_IF` arm. Require the condition to be one of `INT OP INT`, `IDENT OP INT`, or `INT OP IDENT`. Anything else (fn call, struct field, list element, binary op) returns `ERR x86_emit contract not folded: if <cond>`. This catches the 50 silent-miscompile fixtures listed in audit/x86-investigations.oot §4.
3. **while** — add a `KW_WHILE` arm. Require the condition to follow the same `IDENT OP INT` rule as the `if` clause, and require a `KW_LET KW_MUT` init in the same scope. The current x86_emit_loop.oo matcher already enforces this; the contract gate should too.
4. **fn call** — add a `KW_FN` + `LPAREN` + `IDENT` + `RPAREN` detection inside the fn-body scan. If the call has a non-const-foldable argument list (more than 6 args, struct arg, fn arg, list arg) return `ERR x86_emit contract not folded: call <name>`. This catches arity > 6 and non-int/non-string args before the dispatch chain runs.
5. **return** — add a `KW_RETURN` arm. Require the returned expression to be a const-foldable literal, a bare IDENT, or a fn call of an already-attributed function. Anything else (struct field, list index, binary op, ternary) returns `ERR x86_emit contract not folded: return <expr>`. Today a struct-field return is silently miscompiled by x86_emit_fn.oo:194.

---

## 5. Top 3 weakest features with action plan

The five §2 features all have 0/3 coverage. The three with the largest downstream blast radius are:

1. **fn call** — widest blast radius. Every program with more than 6 args, or with a non-int/non-string arg, silently miscompiles or is dropped by the dispatch chain. Action: copy the existing x86_match_snd_call shape (x86_emit_rsi.oo:166) into a new x86_match_fn_call function in a new x86_emit_fn_call.oo; parameterize on arity 0..9; gate on the contract clause from §4 #4. This single addition closes the 0/3 row and unblocks the 7+arg case.
2. **if** — second-largest blast radius. The 50 silent-miscompile fixtures (audit/x86-investigations.oot §4) are dominated by `if` shapes that the 22-arm token walker does not recognize. Action: add the KW_IF clause from §4 #2 to x86_contract_walk_records; when the gate trips, the dispatch in x86_emit.oo:49 prints the error and exits, so the silent-miscompile path is closed before the matcher chain runs. Pair with a new x86_emit_if.oo that mirrors the x86_emit_ctl.oo const-fold policy for runtime conditions.
3. **let binding** — broad blast radius because let is the most common statement in user code. Action: split x86_emit_typed_let.oo into a generic x86_emit_let.oo (this matches the x86_emit_<feature>.oo convention) and a typed-let specialization; add the KW_LET clause from §4 #1; reuse the typed-let matcher as a sub-arm of the generic matcher.

These three additions turn the 0/3 column into 3/3 for the most-hit features and unblock the silent-miscompile rate that the existing audits have documented.
